0,"Full text of ""La langue mbum de Nganha (Cameroun): phonologie...""",https://archive.org/stream/rosettaproject_mdd_detail-1/rosettaproject_mdd_detail-1_djvu.txt,"1. situation de L'objet. Les populations qui designent connnunement leur langue sous le nom de mbum couvrent un assez vaste terrltolre discontinu au centre du Cameroun, avec quelques Indentations en Republique Centraf ricaine et au Tchad.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
1,"(PDF) Phonology and Morphology of Mambay (Niger-Congo, Adamawa)",https://www.academia.edu/30760907/Phonology_and_Morphology_of_Mambay_Niger_Congo_Adamawa_,"Mambay, a Niger-Congo language, is poorly documented and requires detailed phonological and morphological study. The Mambay population is approximately 15,000, primarily in Cameroon and Chad, with unique sociolinguistic challenges.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
2,The Complete List of Indigenous Languages in Chad - 33travels,https://33travels.com/indigenous-languages-in-chad/,"Niger-Congo (Mbum). Mayo-Kebbi Est Region. 25,000.Tupuri. A major Mbum language spoken in the Fianga area of southwestern Chad and in Cameroon. It is a vigorous language with a strong cultural presence, including traditional music and dance.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
3,Mambai,https://docs.verbix.com/EndangeredLanguages/Mambai,"Language code: mcs. Language family: Niger-Congo, Atlantic-Congo, Volta-Congo, North, Adamawa-Ubangi, Adamawa, Mbum-Day, Mbum, Northern, Tupuri-Mambai (SIL classification). Number of speakers: ~15,000.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
4,Mono language (Cameroon) — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/mono_language_cameroon,"The Mono language is spoken by a small ethnic group in northern Cameroon, sharing historical origins with the Dama and Pam peoples as part of the broader Mbum linguistic and cultural cluster within the Adamawa branch of Niger-Congo languages.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
5,Wikitongues | mdd,https://wikitongues.org/languages/mdd/,Linguistic genealogy. Niger-Congo. Mbum resources.Mbum language Videos. Wikitongues crowd-sources video samples of every language in the world.,Mbum,Niger-Congo
6,Talk by Janika Kunzmann (GU) – Institut für Linguistik,https://www.linguistik-in-frankfurt.de/en/talk-by-janika-kunzmann-gu-2/,"Mbum is a Kebi-Benue language classified by Boyd (1989) to be part of the Adamawa family. Like many languages of this group, which is highly disputed in its internal classification (cf. Kleinewillinghöfer 2014), Mbum lacks a detailed phonological description.",Mbum,Niger-Congo
